    "lawCode": "Amd §4223, Ins L",
    "lawSection": "Insurance Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S7744\n\nSPONSOR: BRESLIN\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the insurance law, in relation to authorizing dividend\npaying deferred income annuities\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1. Subparagraph (E) of paragraph (1) of subsection (a) of\nsection 4223 of the insurance law is amended to provide that purchasers\nof annuities that pay a dividend are a separate class of annuitants from\npurchasers of annuities that do not pay a dividend.\n\nSection 2. Effective Date\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThis legislation is necessary to ensure that New York consumers have\naccess to a full array of annuity products, including participating\ndeferred income annuities, which have become increasingly popular in\nrecent years.\n\nIn today's deferred income annuity marketplace, there are a number of\nways that annuities can be customized to suit an individual's needs.\nDeferred income annuities allow individuals to create a pension-like\nincome stream that they can depend on, beginning two years from now or\nfurther into the future. The steady periodic payouts that deferred\nincome annuities provide can help individuals maintain their lifestyle\nafter they retire. An income annuity provides stable lifetime income\nwhich can never be outlived or which may be guaranteed for a specified\nperiod. This is crucially important to individuals who are concerned\nabout outliving their savings. In addition, there is comfort in knowing\ntheir income guarantees are backed by assets of the insurer and not\nsubject to the fluctuations of financial markets.\n\nParticipating (\"Par\") deferred income annuities (\"DIAs\") can be sold in\nevery state except New York. Currently, Par DIAs are not authorized in\nNew York because they cannot pass the \"betterment of rates\" test of New\nYork Insurance Law Section 4223(a)(1)(E). The betterment of rates test\nensures that an annuitant receives the best guaranteed income rate that\nan insurer offers to that class of annuitants at the time annuity income\nstarts. Because Par DIAs pay dividends, guaranteed income rates are set\nat a lower rate (generally 85-90% compared to non-par) than guaranteed\nincome for traditional income annuities, which do not pay dividends.\nBecause of these lowerguarantees, Par DIAs cannot pass the betterment of\nrates test if Par DIA annuitants are considered to be in the same class\nas annuitants on traditional income annuities.\n\nThis bill provides that Par DIA annuitants are a separate class from\nannuitants for a traditional income annuity. This enables Par DIAs to\npass the betterment of rates test because the test is applied only to\nproducts offered to the same class of annuitants. Par DIA guaranteed\nincome would be compared, more appropriately, to that of Participating\nSingle Premium Immediate Annuities ( \"SPIAs\"), rather than to tradi-\ntional (non-par) SPIAs, as currently required by § 4223 (a)(1)(E).\n\n\nNotably, the lower guarantees associated with Par DIAs do not mean lower\ntotal income. Rather, over time, the total income payments (guaranteed\nincome plus dividends) under a Par DIA can easily exceed the fixed\nincome payments under a traditional DIA or SPIA. Annuitants who choose\nthe Par DIA are willing to forego the certainty provided by the higher-\nguarantees of the traditional DIA or SPIA in exchange forthe potential\nof highertotal benefits, over their life expectancy, through the Par\nDIA's guaranteed income plus non-guaranteed dividends. Annuitants with\ndistinctly different income goals and risk tolerances are correctly\nconsidered different classes.\n\nThis bill, by recognizing Par DIA annuitants as a separate class,\nauthorizes the availability of Par DIAs and paves the way for income\nannuity consumers in New York to consider a new product offering that\nmay more closely align with their income goals, retirement horizon, and\nrisk tolerance.\n\nThis newly available product will be subject to all of the rigorous\nrequirements of the New York Insurance Law and Department of Financial\nServices regulations, including comprehensive illustration and disclo-\nsure standards.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nThis is a new bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
