    "lawCode": "Amd §§4223 & 3209, Ins L",
    "lawSection": "Insurance Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S7744A\n\nSPONSOR: BRESLIN\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the insurance law, in relation to authorizing dividend\npaying deferred income annuities\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS::\n\nSection 1. Subparagraph (E) of paragraph (1) of subsection (a) of\nsection 4223 of the insurance law is amended to establish a betterment\nof rates requirement that better reflects the structure of paid-up\ndeferred annuities, including those that pay dividends.\n\nSection 2. A new paragraph (3) is added to subsection (b) of section\n3209 of the insurance law to require certain disclosure related to the\nnon-guaranteed nature of dividends.\n\nSection 3. Effective Date\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThis legislation is necessary to ensure that New York consumers have\naccess to a full array of annuity products, including participating\ndeferred income annuities, which have become increasingly popular in\nrecent years, and to ensure that consumers receive the best guaranteed\nincome rate that an insurer offers to that class of annuitants for\ncomparable contracts at the time annuity income is calculated.\n\nIn today's deferred income annuity marketplace, there are a number of\nways that annuities can be customized to suit an individual's needs.\nDeferred income annuities allow individuals to create an income stream\nthat they can depend on, beginning as early as 13 months from the date\nof issue or further into the future. The steady periodic payouts that\ndeferred income annuities provide can help individuals maintain their\nlifestyle after they retire. An income annuity provides stable lifetime\nincome which can never be outlived or which may be guaranteed for a\nspecified period. This is crucially important to individuals who are\nconcerned about outliving their savings. In addition, there is comfort\nin knowing their income guarantees are backed by assets of the insurer\nand not subject to the fluctuations of financial markets.\n\nThe current betterment of rates requirement in Insurance Law Section\n4223(a)(1)(E) was drafted with accumulation products in mind, where\nincome is calculated at the time income payments commence. With paid-up\ndeferred income annuities, each consideration paid into the contract\npurchases guaranteed paid-up annuity benefits determined at the time the\nconsideration is paid. A new betterment of rates requirement is being\nadded for paid-up deferred income annuities, both fully-guaranteed and\nparticipating, to reflect this unique structure.\n\nNotably, participating (\"Par\") paid-up deferred income annuities\n(\"DIAs\") can be sold in every state except New York. Currently, Par DIAs\nare not authorized in New York because they cannot meet the \"betterment\nof rates\" standard of New York Insurance Law Section 4223(a)(1)(E). The\nbetterment of rates requirement ensures that an annuitant receives the\nbest guaranteed income rate that an insurer offers to that class of\nannuitants at the time annuity income starts.  Because Par DIAs pay\ndividends, guaranteed income rates are set at a lower rate (generally\n85-90% compared to non-par) than guaranteed income for traditional\nincome annuities, which do not pay dividends. As a result of these lower\nguarantees, Par DIAs cannot meet the current betterment of rates stand-\nard.\n\nUnder this amendment, whether the contract is participating or fully\nguaranteed, at the time a contribution is paid into to the contract,\nincluding the purchase of paid-up additions with dividends, the current\npurchase rates for new sales of that contract would be used if higher\nthan the guaranteed rates when the contract was issued.  This will\nensure that the contract holder will receive the best guaranteed income\nrate the insurer offers for comparable contracts to that class of annui-\ntants and a contract holder wanting to make additional contributions\nwill not have to purchase multiple copies of the same contract in order\nto get the current purchase rates. If the insurer no longer sells new\nissue of the contract then the rates of any comparable paid-up deferred\nannuity contract offered by the insurer at that time could be used. For\nexample, if an insurer is no longer issuing the contract holder's Par\nDIA, the current rates of a similar Par DIA would be used.\n\nNotably, the lower guarantees associated with Par DIAs do not mean lower\ntotal income. Rather, over time, the total income payments (guaranteed\nincome plus dividends) under a Par DIA can exceed the fixed income\npayments under a traditional DIA or SPIA. Annuitants who choose the Par\nDIA are willing to forego the certainty provided by the higher guaran-\ntees of the traditional DIA or SPIA in exchange for the potential of\nhigher total benefits, over their life expectancy, through the Par DIA's\nguaranteed income plus non-guaranteed dividends. A disclosure require-\nment is being added to Insurance Law section 3209(b)(3) to ensure that\nconsumers are able to make an informed purchase decision between a Par\nDIA and a fully guaranteed DIA.\n\nPaid-up deferred income annuities, including those that are participat-\ning, will be subject to all of the rigorous requirements of the New York\nInsurance Law and Department of Financial Services regulations, includ-\ning comprehensive illustration and disclosure standards.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY::\n\nThis is a new bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS::\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE::\n\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n",
